{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>We will cross over as shock-troops before YHWH.</b> It should have said אנחנו ('we'), but because the children of Gad were masters of strength and might in all places, to tear the prey of the enemies, and they praised themselves with the phrase חלוצים לפני ה׳ ('shock-troops before YHWH'), therefore they diminished themselves with the word נחנו, by way of humility and lowliness, in the manner of (Exodus 16): ״ונחנו מה״ — this is possible to say by way of the plain sense (peshat)." "<b>Amen.</b> We have received that the beginning of this verse with the letter nun was intentional, like the verse that Jeremiah said (Lamentations 3:42): ״נחנו פשענו ומרינו״ — for there the letter nun was required because of the aleph-bet acrostic, and so too here it was required to begin with nun, for there are eleven verses in Scripture that begin with nun and end with nun, and this is one of them; if so, it could not have said אנחנו and it was necessary to write נחנו. And it is known that the Explicit Name emerges from these eleven verses, and it consists of thirteen letters, and if you mention the verses together with the Name that emerges from them, you will not fear any dread, for it is the Name that is effective against fear." "<b>And behold.</b> The children of Gad were not afraid to dwell in these places across the Jordan eastward, because of the nations that surrounded them, even though they were far from their brothers, the rest of the tribes, for they were armed with two kinds of weaponry: the strength of the body and the strength of merit, and the phrase חלוצים לפני ה׳ ('shock-troops before YHWH') embraces them both. And concerning this they said: ״ואתנו אחוזת נחלתנו מעבר לירדן״ — that is, even though our inherited holding is across the Jordan, we are not afraid of them, for we are shock-troops before YHWH. And so in Joshua it is written (Joshua 6:9): ״והחלוץ עובר לפניהם״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: these are the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, who fulfilled their stipulation. For they trusted in their prowess and strength, in the merit of the precept that was in their hand — in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Deuteronomy 33:20): ״וטרף זרוע״ — 'and he tore the arm,' in the merit of the arm-tefillin; אף קדקד — 'also the head,' in the merit of the head-tefillin."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:37.363843606Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}